course: dictionary
lesson: 8809
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. Take the word shear. «In early summer the sheep are sheared» means their wool is cut off, because sheep and wool are in the sentence.
explain: Shear can also mean to cut or break something off with a strong sideways force. «The bolt sheared off» tells you the bolt snapped under force, not that it was cut neatly. Shears, with an s, are big scissors for hedges, grass or wool.
explain: Shear changes its form too. «Farm sheep must be shorn every year» uses the older past form shorn, and sheared is also correct. When you meet an unknown form, look at the clues: wool, hedges or a bolt will tell you which meaning fits.
fact: Shear means to cut off wool, or to cut or break something off with a strong force.
fact: Shears (plural) are large scissors for cutting hedges, grass or wool.
fact: In science, shear is a force that pushes parts of something sideways in opposite directions, like rubbing your hands together.
fact: «Shear forces» can snap bolts or make rocks slide in an earthquake.
fact: «Wind shear» is dangerous for planes.
exercise: «Sheep are sheared in early summer.» What does sheared mean here? | *Had their wool cut off | Ran away over the hill | Were given a new coat of paint | Were moved into the barn
exercise: «The bolt sheared off under the strain.» What does sheared off mean? | Was painted bright red | *Broke off with a strong sideways force | Was polished until it shone | Melted in the sun
exercise: «Dad used the shears to trim the hedge.» What are shears? | *Big scissors for cutting hedges, grass or wool | A kind of sheep | A farm gate | A type of sheepdog
test: Rubbing your hands together is a good picture of which science idea? | Rain | Gravity | Magnets | *Shear, a sideways force pushing parts in opposite directions
test: «Last year the farmer ___ all the sheep.» Which word fits the gap? | shears | *sheared | shear | shearing
exam: In «Farm sheep must be shorn every year», shorn means: | *Had their wool cut off | Covered in mud | Sold at the market | Fed in the barn
